      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To report on gains in oral health and improved quality of life of 12-13-year-old Nepali schoolchildren five and six years after the introduction of fluoride toothpaste in 1999."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross sectional baseline surveys in 1999 and 2001, and follow up surveys in 2004 and 2005 were multi-stage cluster sampling in design."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Urban and rural schools in Central, Far Western, Mid Western and Western Developmental Regions of Nepal."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "2,770, 12-13-year-olds in 1999 and 1,001, 12-13-year-olds in 2004 were examined regionally. 637 12-13-year-olds from Kathmandu valley and 448 12-13-year-olds from Tansen municipality were examined in 1999. The same schools in Kathmandu valley and Tansen were visited in 2005 and 761 and 482 12-13-years from Kathmandu valley and Tansen were examined. 6,064 8-15-year-olds in 2001 and 1,001 12-13-year-olds in 2004 participated in the collection of information on oral hygiene practice and quality of life."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Examinations were carried out by trained and calibrated examiners using the WHO diagnostic criteria for caries and questionnaires were interview administered by trained interviewers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Advocacy for fluoride toothpaste between 1997 and 2002."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was a 26.6% decline in caries prevalence and 38.0% decrease in 12-13-year old DMFT from 1999 to 2004 throughout four of five regions of Nepal. Approximately 65-75% of the 12-13-year-olds used fluoride toothpaste from 1999 to 2004. School specific data reveals a reduction in DMFT of 43.8% in Tansen and 53.6% in the Kathmandu valley from 1999 to 2005. From 2001 to 2004, report of oral pain decreased by 10%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The most likely reason for the decline in dental caries and reduction in oral pain is the widespread consumption of fluoride toothpaste by the 12-13-year-old schoolchildren."
        }
      ]},
